WEBVTT

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.950
This is Guardrail, a real-time security
platform for blockchain threats.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:08.200
Guards are the customizable security
checks that send alerts,

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:13.750
and clicking into each one shows its alert
status as well as configuration details.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:19.850
To create a guard, there are four methods,
though the top two are recommended.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:25.070
You can select from a catalog of guards or
create a DIY guard with a built-in code editor.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:29.250
Or if you need more guidance, one of the
new features I designed was this

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:33.350
Guard Assistant, which answers questions
and recommends guards for your needs.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:41.530
Choosing from the catalog or from the assistant’s
recommendations brings you to this Create Guard wizard.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:48.510
The fields here differ for each guard, but once
verified, it’s added to your table of guards.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:56.000
In addition to Guards and Create Guard,
other redesigned features include

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.380
Alerts, which notifies
about security incidents,

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.400
Simulate, where guards can be
tested on a transaction,

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:10.960
Response, to set where you want to be
notified and how to respond to threats,

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:16.900
and Addresses,
where your contract addresses live.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:23.660
Other new features I designed include this Dashboard,
which has an overview of any important information,

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:27.240
Screening, to monitor
compliance and security risks,

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.500
and Threat Intelligence, to keep up to date
with any recent attack patterns.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:40.210
As you can see, most of Guardrail is made up of
dense tables and a lot of repeating elements.

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:45.500
The challenge was figuring out how best to
display the dense data to Guardrail’s users

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:50.030
across different screen sizes, while using a
pattern other designers could easily recreate.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:55.390
I worked with Guardrail’s front-end developer
to set up the foundations for our

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:59.410
design system, including color and spacing
tokens, as well as the core components.

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:03.000
In this example of the Guards table,
I decided to set list view as default

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.690
on mobile-sized screens
for better legibility.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:11.430
This became the template we used for future
designs, so all three of us designers

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.070
could maintain consistency
across the different features.

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:19.510
Creating spacing tokens also allowed us
to maintain the right proportions across

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:25.990
desktop and mobile and all the other features without
constantly having to double-check each other’s work.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.980
The color tokens allowed us to support
light and dark mode from the same system.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:45.120
It meant that designers and developers no
longer had to question what color went

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.780
where, and color contrast was always
checked before it touched a screen.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.020
And with this design system, the developers
were able to reduce their custom

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:58.300
styling by 90%, while our design team
could redesign and expand the product.
